#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "a4eeb5c3-c1d5-5eb2-bb4e-03de915e0dab" :question/math-academy-id "e-8579" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1)$.\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ &= p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1) + p(-1)q(-1) \\\\\n&= (1)(0) + (3)(k - 1) + (1)(-k - 1) \\\\\n&= 0 + (3k - 3) + (-k - 1) \\\\\n&= 2k - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n2k - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2k &= 4 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b2d31046-ad1b-5de2-8d2b-19c2ce8b2a0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8580" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= ∫_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(kt^{2} + 3t)(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(kt^{3} + 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= (\\frac{kt^{4}}{4} + t^{3}) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{4} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{4} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{k}{4} &= -1 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "a9ae1458-cfaf-5a3d-a1c8-d7d539480fd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8581" :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3$.\n\nGiven that $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$, find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= 3x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 2x_{3}y_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (2 \\cdot k) + 1 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot (-1)) \\\\\n&= 6k - 2 - 4 \\\\\n&= 6k - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nand solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n6k - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n6k &= 6 \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "f084687c-bbe3-5506-b803-ae2c7e450a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8582" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$.\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n2. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n3. $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta))$." :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2π}\\sin 2t\\cos tdt &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{2π}(\\sin (2t - t) + \\sin (2t + t))dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{2π}(\\sin t + \\sin 3t)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(-\\cos t - \\frac{\\cos 3t}{3}) \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[- \\cos (2π) - (\\cos (6π))/(3)] - \\frac{1}{2}[- \\cos 0 - \\frac{\\cos 0}{3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[- 1 - \\frac{1}{3}] - \\frac{1}{2}[- 1 - \\frac{1}{3}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} {:answer/id #uuid "d0080e9c-95d4-5ed3-af87-271cc9f3c19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132592} {:answer/id #uuid "09b8a0a9-e693-5862-90fd-17e1cab9583d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132593} {:answer/id #uuid "2db798ed-de8d-5f4e-8199-0378fc05ffd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132594} {:answer/id #uuid "53611b71-8da8-53bd-9ff8-777848081934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} :answer-field/id #uuid "0843c47f-c932-5f31-8986-c111c47d29f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132590}] :question/id #uuid "d41fa515-e181-5501-be57-d1516f0b8643" :question/math-academy-id "q-43979" :question/problem "Let $V = C[0, 1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 1]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$.\nIf $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t^{2} + k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowle